    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Why sorting is mandatory"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Unsorted intervals tell you nothing locally. Two intervals next to each other in the input may sit at opposite ends of the timeline, so you would have to compare every pair — O(N²)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Sorted by start time, a single comparison is decisive: everything before the current position is finished with, and everything after starts later. That is what makes one pass sufficient."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The skeleton"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "intervals.sort((a, b) => a[0] - b[0]);\n\nfor (const [start, end] of intervals) {\n  // decide by comparing `start` with the state so far\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The overlap test"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "One comparison underlies the whole chapter. Two intervals `[a1, a2]` and `[b1, b2]` with `a1 <= b1` overlap exactly when:"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "code": "b1  <=  a2          (the later one starts before the earlier one ends)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Whether to use `<=` or `<` depends on whether touching counts as overlapping. `[1,3]` and `[3,5]` share only the instant 3 — are those a clash? For meeting rooms, usually not. For merging number ranges, usually yes. **Decide this before writing code**; it is the most common source of off-by-one bugs in interval problems."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Cost"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time: O(N log N)**, dominated entirely by the sort — the sweep itself is O(N). **Space: O(1)** beyond the output, or O(N) if the sort allocates."
    }
  ],
